  :seon.task/plan "THE SWITCH-OVER, ONE DESIGN (APPROVED by root to build in its order, 2026-11-15 evening, with four notes, folded in below; root ruled only jolt/flow edits this entry's text until the store merges task text by part). Flow leads the design (the owner via root, 2026-11-15 evening); runtime builds; deps builds the library half (Jolt, Datahike, konserve) and owns seon.system/the-store-half-of-an-update-that-loses-nothing; tests owns the binary-test class (item E); root reads this before code. It folds in runtime's steps 0-5 and slice 3a (jolt/runtime 7ebb110f84 and before), deps' half and rails (jolt/deps b3b1fd8f7a, 18fe98d1a9) and flow's half (jolt/flow 16ab36ef90, af9c5a8afd); the earlier texts stay in git. One branch edits this entry's text: jolt/flow (asked of root); the others propose in messages or their own entries.\nRULES (the owner, 2026-11-15; decisions.md 5bdd803d31, a22efe8ce6): state lives only in the db or in Flow procs, both built to start and stop properly; a switch-over is a clean close plus every proc drained; the db is the same after (deps [M result/ad222c41]: data.mdb byte-identical across sync!, close, connect and query; only a new commit changes it); nothing in a channel is the only copy (what must outlive a process is a fact first, docs/flow.md, A fact or a channel); no clock decides anything; a new binary runs beside the old, tests itself, and takes over only if green; red leaves root as it was; no root adopts another root's children; an in-flight eval ends and resumes at its turn boundary from its branch.\nMEASURED [M, 2026-11-15 17:3x]: root's graph 81 procs, 80 in-ports, 0 messages queued, one ping 5.1 ms (result/c7a3bfc3); flow/stop finishes the step in flight and LOSES queued messages (9 of 10), flow/pause keeps them (flow-lab e24); the writer ran one :flushed step 55-70 s at full CPU after a restart (/tmp/flow-writer-hang/, data owns): today's lower bound on any drain; the 14:51 stop overran in stop!'s :drain, which waits with no bound for agent processes to exit (runs/stop-overran-76625); a restart's gap 3 s (stop fact 13:29:31, new process 13:29:34); a second root ~3.8 GB RSS (ps 82278).\nL1 FLOW START AND STOP (the core):\n- JOLT (deps, task jolt.async/a-proc-told-to-drain-finishes-what-is-queued): a lifecycle command ::flow/drain beside pause, resume and stop (a Jolt extension; upstream Flow has none). :draining reads in-ports and control exactly as :running; the transition arity runs once with ::flow/drain on entering it; it ends only by ::flow/stop, which the graph sends; ping shows :draining. Gate red first: told to drain with 9 queued, the proc finishes all 10 (stop loses 9). WHY NOT PAUSE PLUS A READ (root's note 3): pause means take no input, and other callers rely on it (a paused proc's queue is kept for its resume, flow-lab e24); a paused proc that read its in-ports would change upstream's meaning of pause, and a pause has no way to say release what you hold. So drain is written in Flow's own style, pause's shape, fit to offer upstream: one entry in handle-command's map (::flow/drain -> :draining), one case in handle-transition, :draining read as :running in the proc loop, the spi ProcLauncher docstring and flow.clj's process docstring naming it beside pause; a step-fn that ignores the transition (map->step's default returns its state) behaves exactly as when running, so every upstream proc is already correct under it.\n- THE STEP-FN CONTRACT (docs/flow.md#the-step-fn-contract-every-proc-follows): (step state ::flow/drain) releases what the proc holds outside its channels: cancels its armed timers (seon.step/cancel!), answers or hands back its pends typed (seon.step/end-all), stops its self-sends (health-more, put-after! dues), admits no new edge work; its ping map reports :seon.flow/held (work still held, reaching 0 within its bound); its describe declares :seon.flow/drain {:bound ticks}, worked out as arithmetic like any estimate, plus the declared timeouts of any wait on the world it holds (runtime's amendment: a model call or a test child is not ended by ticks but by its own declared bound, :seon.config.fx/bounds' :seon.bound/ms), so a drain's bound is the proc's ticks plus those.\n- BY CONSTRUCTION, WITH A DEFAULT (root's note 4: no list of 81): most procs hold nothing outside their channels, so seon.graph/build gives every proc the default drain (the transition returns its state; :seon.flow/held 0; bound 0) unless its describe declares its own :seon.flow/drain. A proc that holds work must declare it: read from the program index as :stateful-outside-flow reads refs, a step-fn whose code refers to seon.step/pend, ask, ask-edge or put-after!, or forks children, and declares no :seon.flow/drain, is a law's red row (seon.problems/laws), so the default can never hide held work. Only those procs' drains are written by hand. A proc that cannot drain within its bound is the design defect to fix, never a waiver.\n- START is the one start (seon.system/start!): graph/build, flow/start (procs paused), resume; a proc's init derives its state from facts, so nothing is carried across a process.\nL2 THE ROOT DRAIN (seon.system/stop! becomes it; it replaces :drain's unbounded wait):\n1. close the edges: the server answers a new op typed :seon.system/restarting (callers re-ask the next root); the reconciler starts no turn and spawns nothing of its own (refines, judges, stalls: runtime's evidence [M result/c58312c5, runs/stop-overran-37657/ps.txt:423]: with context's round held, the reconciler's refine agent pa1991289846bf34, pid 34438, 99% CPU, 2.3 GB, 6 min into an eval, held the stop's drain); each effects owner admits nothing new (its in-port left out of its read set), the :model owner by G's hold (restart-when-idle);\n2. ::flow/drain to every proc;\n3. quiet: every in-fill 0, no proc :stepping or :writing, every :seon.flow/held 0, read from one ping as the event Jolt's on-quiescent gives, never polled on a clock; the writer's queue is then empty, and it flushes and sync!s (deps' strict order, steps a-b), then answers its :clean-waiters (data's read, writer.clj:593: today the drain path answers only {:op :drained}, so an {:op :clean} asker would hang across the switch-over); the writer holds nothing else a new root cannot derive (data: :conn and :cfg, counters, :program-t seeded at main's t, :union a cache; :pend is step-local; branch writes hold nothing);\n4. end-all answers any waiter left typed (an inbox wait, an edge ask: :seon.system/no-agent);\n5. ::flow/stop; the writer releases its conns (deps' step c). A proc not at :held 0 within its declared bound is ended as a typed error, its state already facts at its boundary: never an unbounded wait. The drain's record is stop!'s own :phases (at a stop the server is closed, so bin/evidence reads only \"root stopped\": runtime): its :drain part names every proc still holding work, each with its :seon.flow/held and its ::flow/site (what it steps, its message, since when), so a drain that overran says what it waited on. An agent mid-eval is never waited for: its drain asks it to stop at its boundary, and past its declared bound (its eval's ticks, plus a declared world wait it holds) root's interrupt ends the eval typed (seon.intr, the agent's REPL), and its turn re-opens from its basis at the next root. Measured [runtime M]: today's two overruns (17:41, and 37657's) held stop!'s :drain 39.5 s and 45.5 s of wall at 76 ticks of work: a pure wait on agent exits, the case this ends.\nWHAT THE PERSON SEES (root's note 2), L2 to 3b: an MCP call that arrives at a draining root answers the typed :seon.system/restarting at once (never a dropped connection), and carries :next-port, the candidate's own port (it listens on its own ports, 3a), so bin/mcp's relay connects there ONCE: the connect succeeds at once (the candidate is up) and the new root holds the call until it has taken over (a wait on its own takeover, the :seon.system/candidate fact), then answers it; no