holds.\n- S5 VALUES A PROCESS DERIVES AT START (config, ctx, contract checks): derived again by the new process; each names its edge or moves into its owner proc.\n- WORK IN FLIGHT IS FACTS ALREADY (flow, runtime's step 4): the reconciler's :flights are derived (relisted from task facts; one in the air is re-decided from its task's outcome); the api's :pending and ::waits end typed at the stop and are asked again; the :model owner's held queue is dropped (a held call never went out). What must be a fact is: a task :running, an agent's turn and basis on its branch.\nL4 THE TAKEOVER, IN ORDER:\n0. Collection off the boot path (runtime; NOT DONE, corrected by runtime [M result/a57a6c1b, c6214539]: boot's retain! still runs at system.clj:1734, and 6 of today's 7 restarts over 60 s were that sweep, 47-74 s each; collect! at quiescence with no agent live exists, so retain!'s sweep moves into collect!'s path and boot's call goes; compact stays at the next boot).\n1. runtime-for decides keep or restart (built, deps reviewed): current loads in place; else a restart names :seon.process/differs.\n2. The update owner (:update, one proc in root; flow reviewed YES with 3): updates are messages, one at a time; the caller waits on the :seon.system/last-update fact (the name since ad0f05702c), never a worker; its queue holds at most the newest.\n3a. THE CANDIDATE (runtime; flow's conditions and deps' rails): built only when runtime-for names a difference; booted beside root on the same store with :seon.config.root/role :candidate, the ONE switch every boot step reads (no drop-trees!, retain!, compact; no write on main; no reconciler, no agent). It is an output of :update to the :process owner (admitted by D at its declared weight); the worker holding it turns its {:outcome :green|:red :digest} into the effect's outcome on :update's own :answers port; that wait ends by the answer, its death (EOF: typed :seon.system/candidate-died), its build's typed error or :update's stop (end-all), never a clock; root writes :seon.system/candidate. Red: the worker stops and reaps it (and calls konserve's reader-check), and root never paused. FIRST, deps' six rails: (a) collect!'s one predicate becomes another process holds the store (an agent or a candidate: the :seon.system/candidate fact), never a second flag; (b) the candidate's tests write branches only with (a), and it drops its own; (c) konserve handles MDB_MAP_RESIZED; (d) compact-if-due! refuses while another process holds the env; (e) the candidate reads main's head from the store again before taking over, never its own conn's; (f) reader-check when a candidate is reaped. And the read-only connect fix in the konserve fork (MDB_IS_READONLY), with deps' unexplained 20 s first connect found first.\n3b. TAKEOVER (green): root runs L2 (the drain) to deps' strict order: (a) the writer's in-port closes, (b) it commits what it took and sync!s, answering {:op :drained} (writer.clj, exists), (c) it releases its conns and root exits; (d) the candidate opens main's writer (LMDB's one write lock is the rail; MDB_USE_ROBUST frees a dead writer's), reads main's head from the store, catches its program up by load-diff! (the replay since its start), binds the api's port once the old has closed it, and starts its reconciler; agents re-fork at their next turn. A crash between (b) and (d) loses nothing; between (a) and (b) only writes taken and not committed, whose senders were answered typed.\n5. The stop never overruns: each proc ends within its declared drain bound; stop!'s :phases name each part's ticks beside its wall.\nCOST: a drain is bounded below by the longest step in flight (today the writer's 55-70 s :flushed step). With the merge off the writer (the :merge fx kind, data) a writer step is its batch's applies, ~37k ticks a checked op [data M], ~0.25 s a step; over that bound until landing 10: :merge-candidate and :branch-diff (the old three-way, 15.3 s stale [data M c6594a16]), :octopus-merge, :collect (only at quiescence) and a :branch-for-tree catch-up tx; quiescence one ping (5 ms); the handoff one flush and sync! plus the new writer's open (lower bound one fsync); the candidate a build only when an input moved (~2 min), one warm boot, the binary-test class (tests [M root result/de3b102b]: 239 tests reach the binary plus 29 that start a process of their own; 896 s summed serially, p50 1.25 s, p90 7.3 s, max 58.6 s; their own ticks 355M in all, the rest the roots and processes they start; ~1-2 min at ~16 workers, bounded below by the 58.6 s test; none writes the live store, each seeds its own test store, so the candidate needs no write to it) and ~3.8 GB of memory for its length (D admits it).\nDELETES, as each lands (with what does its job): stop!'s unbounded :drain wait (L2); bin/update's stop-and-start path and the holds over restarts (3b); boot's retain! (step 0, collect!); the tree-compare stale-binary check (runtime-for). No functionality removed.\nBUILD ORDER: (1) ::flow/drain in Jolt (deps) with the contract, the build refusal and the law (runtime, flow reviews), every proc of root's graph given its drain, S1-S5; (2) L2 replaces stop!'s :drain (its step 2 built: stop! tells every proc ::flow/drain before the api's :shutdown, seon.system/drain-then-shutdown, proof seon.step-test/a-stop-drains-the-graph-before-its-shutdown; steps 1 and 3-5 left); (3) deps' rails a-f and the read-only connect; (4) 3a; (5) 3b.\nDONE WHEN: a pin lands during a watched seon_done and it ends green, and a src-only landing restarts nothing. NEEDS (not yet linked): seon.system/the-store-half-of-an-update-that-loses-nothing is deps' entry on jolt/deps, not yet on main; this entry names it in :seon.task/needs once it lands (a needs naming no task refuses the task file).\nTHE 2026-11-16 RULINGS (flow, agreed with runtime and deps in root's design round): the switch-over is a reactive start. (1) Only facts true when written are stored: a process's liveness (:seon.agent/status's live values, :seon.process/running and :stall) is root's live view, a reaction, never a fact on main, so a restart repairs nothing (it deletes seon.task/earlier-agents-tx, task.clj:360, and record-agents' copy of the view, system.clj:98). (2) What must be a fact is only task :execution and an agent's turn and basis on its branch; the new root's start is a reaction over them: a task :running with no live process is spawned at its turn boundary, and a parked agent owed a message (seon.server/a-message-is-read-by-value-and-marks-nothing) is woken, which deletes the reconciler's :late, settle-late and boot-wakes (reconcile.clj:1450, :1467). (3) A drain then only answers what a proc holds, typed (end-all), and cancels its timers; a parked seon_inbox wait is answered :seon.system/restarting with :next-port, as any op at a draining root. (4) Inside the live env, a branch's one writer is root's writer's own state (granted at spawn, returned at the agent's end), never a fact read from main (writer.clj:314 branch-held reads :seon.agent/status); a candidate opens the env read-only and its binary tests write an env of their own, so one writer per env is LMDB's lock. (5) Step 0's sweep runs as an effect off the writer at quiescence, reading one store value and writing its deletes in batches within the writer's step bound; a drain ends it typed and the next quiescence starts it from the store. (6) One loader: load-diff! is both the takeover's catch-up and a warm branch process's.\nBUILD STEP 1 PLAN (flow, 2026-11-16, root reads before code): the drain contract in Seon's procs. PRIOR WORK, read: Jolt's ::flow/drain is at the pin (flow.clj:174 drain, drain-proc; impl.clj:477 :draining read as :running, the transition arity called with ::flow/drain), used nowhere in src; each Seon step-fn's transition arity already ends its holdings at ::flow/stop (system.clj api-proc: step/end-all :pending ::waits; reconcile.clj: end-all); health's :stateful-outside-flow (problems.clj:638) reads each var's refs from the program index, one seek a var. THE CHANGE, nothing new beside these: (a) the contract: at ::flow/drain a step-fn ends what it holds exactly as at ::flow/stop (step/end-all typed :seon.system/restarting, its timers cancelled, no self-send), since a drained proc then only finishes its queue; most transitions become (#{::flow/stop ::flow/drain} tr). (b) holding work is read, never listed: a proc holds work when its step-fn reaches seon.step/pend, ask, ask-edge, put-after! or arm! through the program's call graph; such a proc whose transition does not end its holdings at ::flow/drain is a health row (:undrained-proc, beside :stateful-outside-flow, same read); a proc that holds nothing needs nothing, so no default wrapper at seon.graph/build. (c) the holders today [read: grep of pend/ask/put-after!/arm!/end-all per proc file]: the api (::pending, ::waits, ::killers, the stall timer), the reconciler (its asks), prove, vectors and render (asks), web (feeds), the effects owners (timers, in-flight), cron (its tick), the fork server (children); each gets its drain in the landing that names it. (d) seon.system/stop! drains (flow/drain), then stops: the stop's unbounded :drain wait goes in step 2 (L2). COST: the law O(holder vars x call-graph depth), ~10 holders, ~1M ticks a health run; a drain O(held) per proc, one ping to see quiet; lower bound one message a proc. PROOFS, red first: seon.problems-test/a-proc-that-holds-work-and-does-not-drain-is-a-row; seon.step-test/a-drain-answers-every-waiter-typed.\nSTEP 1 STATUS (flow, 2026-11-16): built on jolt/flow: the :undrained-proc row; :seon.step/transition admits ::flow/drain (its armed contract threw at a drain, so Flow kept the old state: found when the api reported :draining and answered no wait); the api, reconciler, web and render end their holdings at ::flow/drain as at ::flow/stop. MEASURED: the row on main's program visits 1,917 vars, 110M ticks [M result/ad977787], 100x the ~1M estimated: a proc reaches most of the program. Rows left (10): seon.cron/cron-proc, seon.prove/prove-proc, seon.vector/vectors-proc, seon.fx/owner-proc and worker-proc, seon.fork-server/server-step, seon.agent/test-run-step, seon.repl/agent-step, tests-step and transport-step (the last five run under seon.step/run-loop, which takes no drain yet). STEP 1, SECOND PART (built on jolt/flow, unlanded while lands wait on tests' 11 and 12): seon.step/run-loop takes {:op :drain} (the transition once, then its queue); the fork server drains (root's proc passes the drain to its process, which forks no more: a fork is refused typed :fork-failed, its children going on); cron's tick stops at a drain; the effects owner and worker declare why their work ends by itself (:seon.flow/drain, a reason in the describe, schema'd); an ask is no holding (the asked proc answers it at its own drain), so prove and vectors hold none. S1 BUILT BY FLOW (root moved it off runtime, 2026-11-16): root's agent proc passes a drain to its process, whose :transport drains the agent's graph; there the transport answers every wait on root typed, the :repl proc ends its run as root's :stop does, the :tests proc says why it holds nothing of its own, and a test run forks no child more (seon.step-test/an-agents-procs-drain-their-waits-and-fork-no-more). THE DESIGN AS IT WAS: seon.repl/agent-step at ::flow/drain ends its run at its turn boundary as root's :stop does (drive-run with :seon.turn/stop) and answers its queued requests typed; seon.repl/tests-step and transport-step at ::flow/drain answer the waits of seon.agent/await-reply typed (its :waits table, step/end-all) and cancel their bounds; seon.agent/test-run-step at ::flow/drain stops forking children and lets those running end by their own bound (arm-wall!), as a stop then kills them. NEXT: those drains; the row kept to what moved (root's review: a land's health-moved recomputes only the step-fns whose reached vars it changed, through runtime's reach graph kept per program, so it costs a change, not a program: the 110M full walk then runs only when the program is first read); then (d) stop! drains before it stops.\nTHE STOP THAT NEVER ENDS (root gave flow seon.system-test/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote, 2026-11-16: red on three trees at 11:0x-11:1x, its EXIT 0 missing; passed 4 times alone). Read: stop! derefs the api's :shutdown answer with no bound (system.clj stop!, its :drain part), and the api answers it only at the fork server's :fs-down; the fork server's step (seon.fork-server/server-step) is one of the procs with no drain. [H] the stop waits on the fork server's down, which sometimes never comes; settled by the next red's evidence: the test now keeps a stop's runs (bin/system's stop-overran-<pid> evidence and root.log with ROOT STOPPING's fiber dump) and names them in its failure. The fix is L2 at its cause: every proc drains within its bound, the fork server's included, and stop!'s :drain wait goes.\nL2 PLAN, ONE BOUND FOR A STOP (root's review, 2026-11-16: two bounds for one stop is the class): today bin/system kills at :seon.config.root/stop-ms (3,000) while Flow's stop waits :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms (10,000), and stop!'s :drain waits on the api's :shutdown with no bound at all. DESIGN: one key, :seon.config.root/stop-ms, the whole stop's bound; flow-stop-ms is deleted, Flow's :stop-timeout-ms is what of stop-ms the :drain part left (stop! passes it: Jolt's flow/stop takes the graph's, so the graph is made with stop-ms and stop! bounds its own parts inside it). stop! runs its parts against one deadline taken from stop-ms at its start: :fact, then :drain (flow/drain to every proc, then the api's :shutdown answer, deref'd for what is left, past it the typed :seon.system/stop-overran naming the part and each proc still :held from one ping), then :flow-stop for what is left. bin/system reads the root's own bound from ROOT LISTENING (as today) and kills only past it, so one value decides both, and the root always answers its own overrun first. Each proc's drain bound (the plan's :seon.flow/drain {:bound}) is not declared yet; until it is, stop-ms is the one bound and the overrun names the proc that held it. COST: a stop O(procs) messages and one ping; no new wait. PROOF, red first: seon.system-test/a-stop-past-its-bound-answers-the-part-it-was-in (a proc held past the bound: the stop ends within stop-ms, typed, naming it). Deletes: :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms and stop!'s unbounded :drain wait."
