op-overran-40284, root.log.prev of 03:21) [read]: the stop wrote NO :phases (it was killed before its end), so its own record is missing, a first; at the signal fiber 0 was running (33,390,432,236 ticks and 346 GB over its life, the most of any fiber) and its frames were never printed (a running fiber prints them at its next safe point, which the kill came before), and fiber 1 had just begun an [:api :back] step. So: (1) the stop must write its :phases incrementally (each phase as it ends), so a killed stop still names the phase it died in; (2) the dump must name a running fiber's current step (::flow/site) at once, never only at a safe point; (3) the wait itself is flow's L2 drain (seon.system/an-update-loses-nothing). Proof to add: a stop killed mid-drain leaves its finished phases on disk."
