 {:seon.task/name "the-store-half-of-an-update-that-loses-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "DONE 2026-11-16 (deps): (c) a store opens at the map size it was made with (konserve 2bceb36); (d) compaction only under MDB_ALONE (LMDB b4d2b48), sole-writer? no longer decides it; (f) konserve's reader-check deleted (LMDB clears a dead process's slot). LEFT: (a), (b), (e), runtime's. BUILD PLAN (deps, 2026-11-16, root's order after the reuse-sweep fix and ticks) [read: mdb.c mdb_env_excl_lock 6342-6384: every holder keeps a shared fcntl lock on the lock file's byte 0, only a first opener holds it exclusive]. (d) LMDB fork: an open flag (MDB_ALONE) under which mdb_env_open takes the store only when no other process holds it (the exclusive lock, never the shared fallback), keeps it exclusive until close, and answers EBUSY otherwise, so no process can open the store mid-compaction; konserve's open-env passes it and answers the typed {:type :lmdb-not-alone}; seon.db/compact-if-due! opens with it, and a refusal is no compaction now (the next boot asks again); :seon.config.store/sole-writer? no longer decides compaction (system.clj boot! 1671). (c) konserve's open-env adopts the store's own map size after open (mdb_env_set_mapsize 0) and answers MDB_MAP_RESIZED on a txn by adopting it and retrying once; :seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes sizes only a store being made, and a larger map is an explicit grow. COST: O(1) a open (one fcntl, one set_mapsize); compaction as today. PROOFS red first: lmdb-kill case c (an ALONE open refused while another process holds the store, granted after it closes); seon.db-test/a-store-another-process-holds-is-not-compacted; seon.db-test/a-store-opens-at-the-map-size-it-was-made-with. RULINGS APPLIED (2026-11-16, design round; agreed with flow, message to deps): an unstable state is made impossible, never guarded. (d) compaction leaves boot: it runs only where LMDB's exclusive first open shows no other process holds the env, so no candidate can rename data.mdb under root, and sole-writer? stops deciding it (system.clj boot! 1671; db.clj compact-if-due! 1019). (c) the map size is the store's, read from its meta at open, never each binary's config, so two binaries cannot differ on it (konserve lmdb_ffi.jolt open-env sets it once). The candidate opens the live env MDB_RDONLY and its binary tests write in an env of their own, so one writer per env is LMDB's own lock; inside the live env a branch's one writer is root's writer's state, never a fact (flow). A writer outside root's syncs grows the file by at most :seon.config.store/lmdb-sync-pages (task seon.system/a-write-before-the-writer-syncs-its-own-steps, on jolt/deps). (a), (e), (f) as below. EARLIER: Owner: deps (runtime owns the spec: seon.system/an-update-loses-nothing names this entry in its :seon.task/needs). REVIEW OF RUNTIME'S SLICE 3a (deps, 2026-11-15 17:5x; a candidate root on the live LMDB, :seon.config.store/sole-writer? false, skipping compact-if-due!, retain! and drop-trees!, writing no head on main). SAFE AS IT STANDS: reading main. A connect writes nothing (byte-identical, above); LMDB serializes writers across processes; konserve's reads are short MDB_NOTLS read txns; the live root's sweep (seon.db/collect!, datahike gc-storage with (Date. 0)) keeps every branch's history, so no commit of main the candidate reads is swept. NOT SAFE YET, six things first: (a) [runtime] the LIVE root's collect! sweeps as sole writer while a candidate lives: it refuses only while an agent is live, and sole-writer? is a place key fixed at its boot. gc-storage lists the branches, then sweeps: a candidate commit between the two loses its nodes, leaving a head that names swept nodes. Rail: collect!'s one predicate becomes 'another process holds the store' (an agent or a candidate: the :seon.system/candidate fact), never a second flag. (b) [runtime] the candidate's tests write branches into the live store: only with (a), and the candidate drops its own (root's drop-trees! must not see them as left by an earlier root). (c) [deps] konserve has no MDB_MAP_RESIZED handling (lmdb_ffi.jolt: set_mapsize only at open): a candidate whose :seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes differs grows the map at its first commit and the live root's next txn fails. Rail: konserve answers MAP_RESIZED with mdb_env_set_mapsize 0 and a retry (deps' fork). (d) [deps] compact-if-due! renames a fresh data.mdb over the store (db.clj): any process still holding the old file writes to an unlinked inode and loses it. The candidate skipping it is a convention; the rail is in compact-if-due!: it refuses while another process holds the env (LMDB's reader table, mdb_reader_list's pids, or the exclusive lock LMDB takes for its first opener). At 3b the new root compacts only after the old one has exited. (e) [runtime] the candidate's conn sees main as of its connect: Datahike's conn advances only by its own writer's commits, so 3b's catch-up reads main's head from the store again after the old root's sync!, never the candidate's @conn. (f) [deps] a candidate killed mid-read can leave LMDB reader slots that pin old pages, so the file grows: konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi/reader-check (lmdb_ffi.jolt:236) has no caller; root calls it when it reaps a candidate. Also open: (2)'s 20 s wait above. DEPS' HALF: THE STORE AND THE LIBRARIES (deps, 2026-11-15 17:4x; root's ask for the owner's model: drain, clean shutdown, the same db). (1) HANDING THE WRITER AND LMDB OVER. What a commit is [read]: on LMDB a whole Datahike commit (its index nodes, schema-meta, graph records, changes, its record, then the branch head) is ONE LMDB write transaction (datahike.writing/write-commit!, k/multi-assoc, writing.cljc:686-708), so a crash anywhere leaves the previous commit or this one, never a head naming a missing value. Seon opens every store MDB_NOSYNC|MDB_SAFENOSYNC (seon.db/store-cfg, db.clj:649): a commit survives a process crash (the OS page cache holds it) and an OS crash rolls back to the last sync, never corrupting (our fork's SAFENOSYNC; bin/check's lmdb-crash and lmdb-kill steps, docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-crash-safety). mdb_env_close only unmaps (mdb.c mdb_env_close_active: munmap, no sync), so durability at the handover is an explicit seon.db/sync! (db.clj:667). THE STRICT ORDER: old root (a) stops taking writes: the writer proc's in-port closes; (b) the writer flushes: it commits what it took, seon.db/sync! when dirty, and answers {:op :drained} (writer.clj:521-524, exists today); (c) releases its conns (konserve closes the env with its last holder, lmdb.jolt:131) and exits; (d) the new process opens the store and takes the writer role. LMDB's write lock is the rail (one writer per env across processes, MDB_USE_ROBUST: a writer that dies releases it), so new cannot write before old's last commit is in. A crash between (b) and (d) loses nothing: every commit is in, and the steady snapshot is the last; between (a) and (b), the writes taken but not committed are the only loss, which runtime's spec answers (their senders are told, or they are replayed). BYTE-IDENTICAL [M root result/ad222c41, scratch store /tmp/deps-handover-*, 20 commits, 1.5 MB]: data.mdb's sha after sync! and close c4cf6b23, the same after a connect and a query, and after closing again; only a new commit changes it (d5c89b7a). A connect writes nothing. (2) A NEW BINARY READ-ONLY BESIDE THE WRITER. LMDB: yes. LMDB's own mdb_stat, built against Seon's liblmdb (-DMDB_USE_ROBUST: another build's lock format is MDB_VERSION_MISMATCH), opened the live 4.1 GB runs/store read-only beside root's writer: 0.37 s wall, 2 MB RSS (340,632 pages of 16 KB; 5 of 126 reader slots used) [M 17:3x]. Datahike: NOT TODAY: a connect with :lmdb :flags MDB_RDONLY fails 'MDB_IS_READONLY' in konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi/open-env (lmdb_ffi.jolt:62), which opens the main dbi in a WRITE txn; the fix is ours, one line in the konserve fork (begin that txn MDB_RDONLY when the env is), then a pin [M root result/d340e9fa]. COST of a reader beside: its own mmap of pages it touches (shared page cache, no copy), one reader slot, and the snapshot it holds keeps the pages it reads from reuse until its read txn ends: a test run holding one read txn for minutes grows the file by what the writer commits meanwhile; each test's reads must be short txns (konserve's are: one per read). A write needs LMDB's one write lock, which the running writer holds, so a read-only boot runs only tests that read; tests that write go on a copy (mdb_copy -c, a compacting copy of the live env, or the fork server's per-test stores). UNEXPLAINED: the eval that first ran the read-only connect beside a C process holding the write lock took 20 s wall while the connect itself measured 0 ms (result/c459cc69): to find before (2) is relied on. (3) JOLT-SIDE STATE THAT DOES NOT SURVIVE A PROCESS (read): fibers and their continuations, channels and every Flow proc's state (in memory: they are restarted, and their durable state is what the db holds); the io-poller's kqueue and thread (not even inherited by fork: jolt-io-after-fork, fibers-async.ss:714); the fork server and every agent forked from it, which end with root (seon.lib/end-with!: a process ends with what started it), so agents mid-turn die unless the spec keeps them; open native handles: LMDB envs (closed by exit; robust mutexes and the reader table clean up after a dead pid), SQLite text indexes (rebuilt from their on-disk images by content key: survive as files), the embedding model's worker, sockets to agents and the MCP clients (closed: clients reconnect); Datahike's node cache and the reactive core's reactions (cold: rebuilt on first read; a reaction's keys come back only by rerunning its reader, docs/storage.md#what-a-read-scanned). What survives: the store (byte-identical), every file under target/ (compile caches, text-index images), the config and the program in the db. "
  :seon.task/kind :work
