 {:seon.task/name "a-gates-candidate-carries-the-branchs-own-task-entries", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-gate-is-proved-by-the-system-on-its-candidates-checkout}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.system/prove-candidate! {:n 1, :order :linear, :ticks 5000000, :bytes 20000000}}, :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow (seon.system/prove-candidate!, the merge gate's candidate tree). Found by data's pilot, 2026-11-16 [M result/cc557d8b]: task seon.db/an-eval-responses-parts-are-its-schemas-keys deletes seon.db/result-parts; its plan in the store was fixed to name the deleted var in two backticks, but the gate's candidate tree takes task FILES from main (a task changed in the store is not written back to its file yet: seon.source/a-task-changed-in-the-db-is-written-back-to-its-file), and main's entry cited the var, so the candidate's seed refused it (:seon.db/no-target in seon.system/seed-program!) and all 281 process tests answered :error. The class: any branch that deletes a var its own task's file entry cites fails the gate's seed, whatever its store holds. FIX: the candidate tree writes the branch's own task entries (the task files the merge would write), as it writes its program files. The proof to write, red first: a branch deleting a var its task's file entry cites passes the gate's seed. Its proof extends seon.fx-test/a-gate-is-proved-by-the-system-on-its-candidates-checkout."}
 {:seon.task/name "a-booting-root-answers-booting-never-a-silent-connection"
  :seon.task/plan "ONE READINESS (runtime, 2026-11-16 audit: two tasks held one job; seon.system/status-says-up-only-once-the-root-listens folded in here): readiness is root's own answer, never a shell's reading of files. bin/system status says up whenever root.pids names a live process and makes up the configured port when root.port is missing (bin/system:163), so between a fork and ROOT LISTENING it says up on a port nothing listens on. A shell fix (status reads root.port, exit 2 while starting) was built and reverted on jolt/runtime (160237fce5, cc53cbce8e; reverted 68a86fec4a, bfad94f85e): with it, seon.agent-test/a-process-test-gets-its-verdict-while-the-agent-can-act and a-process-test-green-at-its-digest-asks-no-gate went red three runs in a row (green without it, target/prove/20261116T074220-10783), cause not found, and seon.test-store/start-root! reads the status exit (test_store.clj:456). The inside design: root answers an op asked during boot typed :seon.system/booting at once, and bin/system status is a thin client of that op (up, booting, or none), so every reader (bin/update, bin/deps-probe, bin/worktrees, start-root!) reads one answer. Proof added: seon.check-test/status-says-up-only-once-the-root-listens. Owner: runtime; root's order 2026-11-16 03:5x, after seon.render.proc/a-health-ask-waits-for-settling-never-loops-in-the-step. Flow's observation [H]: after the 03:26 boot, runs/root.port was written at 03:26:37-39 but flow's attach answered only ~15 min later, while an attach takes 2 s once root is up. So root may write its port (bin/system's readiness, ROOT LISTENING) before its ops answer (the boot's catch-up still running), and a client connects and blocks with no word. Runtime read 03:2x: root.log's first line is ROOT LISTENING and the log carries no timing of when ops began answering, so it cannot settle it; runtime's own attach at 03:26:40 returned, its time not taken [H]. Settle: the boot log names each phase's end with its time (the stop's :phases, at boot), and an attach timed against the port's write. If true, the fix is one of: the port is written once ops answer (the server binds when the api is ready); or an op asked during boot answers typed :seon.system/booting at once (a wait on our own system ends with a value or a typed error). PRIOR WORK, to read first: seon.main's ROOT LISTENING, bin/system's ready wait, seon.server's accept loop, seon.system/boot!'s phases. COST: none on a booted root."
