 {:seon.task/name "a-write-before-the-writer-syncs-its-own-steps", :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. CAUSE CLASS (measured): under our LMDB fork's MDB_SAFENOSYNC the steady snapshot (the last forced sync) is held as a reader would be (mdb.c mdb_find_oldest), so a writer that commits without a sync reuses no freed page. MEASURED 2026-11-16 (deps) on a clone of runs/store compacted by Seon's own compact-if-due! (991,333 pages): 300 commits of 200 datoms each, synced after each, grew it 11,102 pages (37 a commit); the same 300 unsynced grew it 601,619 pages (~9.4 GB, ~2,000 a commit), 54x. BOOT IS NOT IT (measured, same day): a root booted on a clone of runs/store (deps build, provider :none) wrote nothing in open!, drop-trees! or retain!'s mark (last txn flat at 1644 for 6.5 min); its sweep and every later boot write kept the steady txn within 1-3 of the last (probe sampled at 0.2 s, read from the lock file's mti_steady); a session attach, 6 txns, all synced. The recorded growths: 03:02 to 03:21, root 40284 (stop overran at 03:21), ~330 txns took ~658k pages, ~2,000 a txn, the unsynced rate; 05:16 to 05:18:55, 305 txns took 124k pages, 407 a txn; both on a just-compacted file (empty free list), both stopped when the next root opened and steadied. So the hypothesis is a running root whose syncs stopped (the writer's :notified held true with no :flushed coming back, or the flusher's sync blocked), or a reader slot pinning an old snapshot; the probe below names which at the next occurrence. CHANGE (the class, not one writer): our LMDB fork bounds what a process may commit unsynced: mdb_env_set_syncpages(env, n), and a top-level commit under MDB_SAFENOSYNC that brings the pages this process wrote since the steady snapshot to n makes a steady point itself (mdb_env_steady), as libmdbx's MDBX_opt_sync_bytes does (the field's way). konserve's lmdb_ffi takes :sync-pages at open-env and gains env-info (mdb_env_info: last txn, pages used, map pages), which seon.db/a-store-near-its-map-is-compacted-at-boot also needs; seon.db/store-cfg passes :seon.config.store/lmdb-sync-pages (a spec'd key, its reason: the file grows by at most this many pages a writer between syncs). DELETES: seon.db/retain!'s :konserve.gc/batch-done sync (the bound covers it), and the docs' rule that a write outside the writer syncs its own steps (storage.md#durability-is-continuous-group-commit), which the bound makes true by construction. The writer's flusher stays: it makes commits durable before an outside effect, which the bound does not. PRIOR WORK: seon.db/sync!, seon.writer flusher-proc (writer.clj 560-578), retain!'s batch-done sync (db.clj 1118-1121), open!'s sync after a seed; the fork's mdb_env_steady (mdb.c 3455) and mdb_env_sync's SAFENOSYNC branch (3628); libmdbx sync_bytes; searched git grep 'sync' in src/seon and the tasks, no bound found. COST: estimate per top-level commit one add and one compare (O(1), <100 ticks); one fdatasync (~5-20 ms) per n pages written unsynced, none while the writer's flusher keeps up; the bound n=4096 pages (64 MiB) caps an unsynced writer's growth at 64 MiB a process instead of ~32 MiB a commit. PROOFS, red first: native lmdb test (bin/build's lmdb-* family): 300 commits under SAFENOSYNC with no sync grow last_pgno by under 2n with syncpages n, and by >100n without; seon.db-test/a-write-outside-the-writer-grows-the-file-by-at-most-its-bound: a conn on a test store, 300 commits never synced, pages used (env-info) grows under the bound's share. EVIDENCE (deps, 2026-11-16, read-only probe of the pinned fork's mdb_env_info and the lock file's mti_steady, sampled 1 s on runs/store and 0.2 s on a clone): root 90872 kept last txn == steady txn at every sample 07:29-08:02, the largest gap 21 txns; a root booted on a clone of runs/store (25.77 GB, provider :none) wrote nothing in open!, drop-trees! or retain!'s mark (txn 1644 flat 6.5 min), and its sweep and every later write kept steady within 1-3 txns of the last. Not the same cause as the switch-over's step 0 (collection off the boot path): that one is a sweep under another process; this one is any unsynced writer. OPEN (root's note 1, 2026-11-16): a writer whose syncs stop for minutes is its own bug (a wait that never ends). Seen from the runtime once this pin lands: root's state shows :store :unsynced-txns (seon.system/state over seon.db/map-use, which reads the env's last and steady txns; the /tmp probe that measured the evidence above is deleted). When it shows one running ahead, its cause (the flusher blocked, the writer's :notified held with no :flushed, a pinned reader) is filed for its owner, the writer's being data's.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-sweep-at-boot-reuses-the-pages-it-frees seon.db-test/a-write-outside-the-writer-grows-the-file-by-at-most-its-bound}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.db/store-cfg {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 100000, :bytes 10000}}}
